1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,198
Ask a class two questions.

2
00:00:02,198 --> 00:00:03,266
Who likes tea?

3
00:00:03,266 --> 00:00:04,476
Who likes coffee?

4
00:00:04,476 --> 00:00:08,908
Add the two answers, and you can get
more students than the class has.

5
00:00:09,308 --> 00:00:10,271
Nobody lied.

6
00:00:10,271 --> 00:00:12,862
Some students were simply counted twice.

7
00:00:13,312 --> 00:00:15,824
Diya ran this survey for the class trip.

8
00:00:16,174 --> 00:00:18,169
Twenty five students like tea.

9
00:00:18,169 --> 00:00:19,609
Eighteen like coffee.

10
00:00:19,609 --> 00:00:20,709
Ten like both.

11
00:00:20,709 --> 00:00:23,161
And every student likes at least one.

12
00:00:23,161 --> 00:00:25,262
So how many students are there?

13
00:00:25,762 --> 00:00:26,918
Not forty three.

14
00:00:26,918 --> 00:00:29,388
Sets give the right count in one line.

15
00:00:30,038 --> 00:00:31,256
First, a symbol.

16
00:00:31,256 --> 00:00:34,674
n of A means the number of
elements in a finite set A.

17
00:00:35,074 --> 00:00:38,269
Let T be the tea lovers,
and C the coffee lovers.

18
00:00:38,269 --> 00:00:39,645
Now watch the count.

19
00:00:40,045 --> 00:00:41,972
Count every student in T.

20
00:00:41,972 --> 00:00:43,011
Twenty five.

21
00:00:43,411 --> 00:00:46,150
Now count every student in C, and add.

22
00:00:46,150 --> 00:00:47,123
Forty three.

23
00:00:47,523 --> 00:00:49,131
But look at the overlap.

24
00:00:49,131 --> 00:00:52,780
Those ten students were counted
once in T, and again in C.

25
00:00:53,180 --> 00:00:54,695
Take them away once.

26
00:00:54,695 --> 00:00:57,826
Forty three minus ten is
thirty three students.

27
00:00:58,476 --> 00:01:00,592
Now let's turn this into a formula.

28
00:01:00,942 --> 00:01:01,842
Step one.

29
00:01:01,810 --> 00:01:06,150
Adding n of A and n of B counts
every element of A union B.

30
00:01:06,900 --> 00:01:07,800
Step two.

31
00:01:07,685 --> 00:01:12,315
But the elements of A intersection B
get counted twice, once in each set.

32
00:01:13,065 --> 00:01:14,043
Step three.

33
00:01:14,043 --> 00:01:15,732
So subtract them once.

34
00:01:15,732 --> 00:01:21,193
n of A union B equals n of A, plus
n of B, minus n of A intersection B.

35
00:01:21,943 --> 00:01:22,852
Step four.

36
00:01:22,852 --> 00:01:26,248
If A and B are disjoint,
the overlap is empty.

37
00:01:26,248 --> 00:01:30,009
Then n of A union B is
simply n of A plus n of B.

38
00:01:30,759 --> 00:01:31,910
Side by side.

39
00:01:31,910 --> 00:01:34,594
Likes tea means everyone in circle T.

40
00:01:34,594 --> 00:01:36,181
That is twenty five.

41
00:01:36,581 --> 00:01:40,941
Likes only tea means T minus C,
the part outside the overlap.

42
00:01:40,941 --> 00:01:43,333
Twenty five minus ten is fifteen.

43
00:01:43,933 --> 00:01:45,008
Now you try.

44
00:01:45,008 --> 00:01:47,470
How many students like only coffee?

45
00:01:47,470 --> 00:01:48,806
Pause and decide.

46
00:01:52,006 --> 00:01:52,906
Eight!

47
00:01:52,616 --> 00:01:56,168
Eighteen like coffee, but
ten of them also like tea.

48
00:01:56,168 --> 00:01:58,120
Eighteen minus ten is eight.

49
00:01:58,770 --> 00:01:59,820
Remember this.

50
00:01:59,820 --> 00:02:03,099
Add the two sets, then
subtract the overlap once.

51
00:02:03,799 --> 00:02:05,995
Check it with the three pieces.

52
00:02:05,995 --> 00:02:07,612
Fifteen like only tea.

53
00:02:07,612 --> 00:02:08,760
Ten like both.

54
00:02:08,760 --> 00:02:10,428
Eight like only coffee.

55
00:02:10,828 --> 00:02:13,700
Fifteen plus ten plus
eight is thirty three.

56
00:02:13,700 --> 00:02:16,816
The same answer, counted
with no student twice.

57
00:02:17,466 --> 00:02:19,202
Now, one worked example.

58
00:02:19,552 --> 00:02:23,839
In a group of sixty students, thirty
five play cricket, twenty eight play

59
00:02:23,839 --> 00:02:25,717
football, and ten play both.

60
00:02:25,717 --> 00:02:27,174
How many play neither?

61
00:02:27,674 --> 00:02:28,574
Step one.

62
00:02:28,526 --> 00:02:31,103
Let C be cricket and F be football.

63
00:02:31,103 --> 00:02:36,059
n of C is thirty five, n of F is
twenty eight, and n of C intersection

64
00:02:36,059 --> 00:02:36,974
F is ten.

65
00:02:37,724 --> 00:02:38,624
Step two.

66
00:02:38,528 --> 00:02:42,678
n of C union F is thirty five,
plus twenty eight, minus ten.

67
00:02:42,678 --> 00:02:44,140
That is fifty three.

68
00:02:44,890 --> 00:02:45,790
Step three.

69
00:02:45,785 --> 00:02:48,838
So fifty three students
play at least one game.

70
00:02:49,588 --> 00:02:50,497
Step four.

71
00:02:50,497 --> 00:02:53,455
Neither means the complement of C union F.

72
00:02:53,455 --> 00:02:55,817
Sixty minus fifty three is seven.

73
00:02:56,567 --> 00:02:58,660
So seven students play neither game.

74
00:02:59,310 --> 00:03:00,775
Now, the exam trap.

75
00:03:01,125 --> 00:03:02,644
Many students just add.

76
00:03:02,644 --> 00:03:05,403
Thirty five plus twenty
eight is sixty three.

77
00:03:05,403 --> 00:03:08,505
That is more than the sixty
students in the group!

78
00:03:08,905 --> 00:03:11,507
The ten who play both were counted twice.

79
00:03:11,507 --> 00:03:14,634
Subtract them once, and
the union is fifty three.

80
00:03:15,284 --> 00:03:16,781
Where is this asked?

81
00:03:16,781 --> 00:03:21,322
In JEE Main, as survey questions with
numbers or percentages, where you

82
00:03:21,322 --> 00:03:25,239
find the overlap, or its least
and greatest possible value.

83
00:03:25,839 --> 00:03:27,125
Exam level now.

84
00:03:27,125 --> 00:03:29,534
Between which values must x lie?

85
00:03:29,534 --> 00:03:30,874
Pause and solve.

86
00:03:36,274 --> 00:03:37,576
The answer is A.

87
00:03:37,576 --> 00:03:39,756
From thirty seven to sixty two.

88
00:03:40,256 --> 00:03:43,332
The union can be at most
one hundred percent.

89
00:03:43,332 --> 00:03:47,967
So sixty two, plus seventy five,
minus x, is at most one hundred.

90
00:03:48,467 --> 00:03:50,490
That gives x at least thirty seven.

91
00:03:50,990 --> 00:03:54,476
And the overlap can never be
bigger than the smaller set.

92
00:03:54,476 --> 00:03:56,302
So x is at most sixty two.

93
00:03:56,902 --> 00:03:58,359
Here is your recap.

94
00:03:58,359 --> 00:03:59,856
Screenshot this card.

95
00:04:03,056 --> 00:04:05,892
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

96
00:04:06,292 --> 00:04:07,192
One.

97
00:04:06,854 --> 00:04:09,760
Write the formula for n of A union B.

98
00:04:10,660 --> 00:04:11,560
Two.

99
00:04:11,186 --> 00:04:14,832
If A and B are disjoint,
what is n of A union B?

100
00:04:15,732 --> 00:04:16,632
Three.

101
00:04:16,360 --> 00:04:21,767
If n of A is twelve, n of B is nine,
and n of A union B is seventeen, what

102
00:04:21,767 --> 00:04:23,682
is n of A intersection B?

103
00:04:24,782 --> 00:04:28,287
Next time: the canteen has
three snacks and two drinks.

104
00:04:28,287 --> 00:04:30,769
How many different combos can you pick?

105
00:04:30,769 --> 00:04:33,545
We find out with Cartesian
products of sets.
